  year =         2000,
  volume =       1843,
  series =       {{LNCS}},
  pages =        {3--19},
  annote =       {The authors present another extension to the original
                  Condensation algorithm {\textbackslash}citeisard:98:a. Using
                  partitioned sampling can effectively reduce the computational
                  demands for a given problem. In principle, partitioned
                  sampling is a statistical analogy
                  {\textbackslash}emphhierarchical search. Articulated objects
                  are therefore suitable in particular for this kind of
                  (multi-)object tracker. Besides partitioned sampling, the
                  paper introduces the notion of {\textbackslash}emphsurvival
                  diagnostics and {\textbackslash}emphsurvival rate. Both
                  quantities can be used for assessing the efficacy of a
                  particle filter. Survival diagnostic – a.k.a.
                  {\textbackslash}empheffective sample set size
                  {\textbackslash}citedoucet:02:a – measures the effective
                  sample set size which has to be within the boundaries of 1
                  (one sample has weight 1 all others have weight 0) and n (all
                  samples have equal weight 1/n). The survival rate is a
                  property of a given sample set meassuring the over-all
                  tracking performance. More technical details about partitioned
                  sampling for hand tracking can be found in
                  {\textbackslash}citeisard:00:a.},
  url =
                  {http://link.springer.de/link/service/series/0558/bibs/1843/18430003.htm},
